            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("63"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Waterways are an analogy: each channel represents a partition and each labelled boat a record. Channel width is not executor capacity; the unequal record counts illustrate skew, not measured traffic or timing. AQE may split eligible physical work even though ordinary hash partitioning routes equal keys together. AQE skew join optimization uses shuffle statistics and configured criteria, and is not guaranteed for every join type/plan. Supported skewed sort-merge work can be split with replication. Increasing partition count alone does not divide one hash key. Broadcast of a genuinely small lookup can avoid a join-key shuffle of the large side; it does not guarantee that pre-existing input skew disappears. Manual salting requires distributing hot-key rows and matching replication with careful correctness checks; keep implementation for advanced reference. Source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html",
    )

pub def slide_join_strategies() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="join-strategies",
        title="How Spark executes a join",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide join-mechanics jm-overview",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("How Spark executes a join", level=1),
            group([
            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                code("sales.join(products, on=\"product_id\", how=\"left\")"),
            ], style="language-python"),
            paragraph([
                code("how=\"left\""),
                text(" defines which rows to keep. Spark still has to choose an execution strategy."),
            ]),
        ], style="jm-overview-request"),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Physical strategy"),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="How it matches rows"),
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Where the work comes from"),
                ]),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Sort-merge join"),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Order each partition’s inputs, then merge equal keys."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Required shuffles and local sorting."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Shuffled hash join"),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Build and probe a lookup per join partition."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Required shuffles and task memory."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Broadcast hash join"),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Probe a complete broadcast lookup."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Distributing the lookup and holding it in memory."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Broadcast nested-loop join"),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Test rows against broadcast candidates."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Many pair comparisons, even after broadcasting."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Cartesian product"),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Process each pair of input partitions."),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Repeated input reads and many candidate pairs."),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="jm-overview-table"),
            prose("The condition and join type limit the choices. Spark also considers statistics, hints and input layout. AQE can revise eligible plans.", style="jm-overview-foot"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("64"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Pivot immediately after skew: “We have chosen the join type in our SQL or DataFrame code. Now we are looking at the mechanics Spark can choose to execute it.” Keep the logical operation and the physical operator distinct. These are alternatives, not five consecutive phases. The first three detailed slides use the same left equality join; the later two explicitly change the condition to explain candidate-pair execution.

Walk down the list before entering any animation. Sort-merge and shuffled hash need compatible distributions, with exchanges when the current layout does not provide them. They differ in local matching. Broadcast hash changes the distribution requirement by making a complete build-side lookup available at participating executors. Nested-loop and Cartesian are distinct ways of processing candidate pairs. CartesianProduct also has the name shuffle-and-replicate nested loop; do not equate that name with the earlier hash-by-key shuffle. This is an introductory list of Spark SQL physical join families, not a decision tree or a ranked speed comparison. Broadcast applicability and build side depend on the logical join type. Non-equality conditions can use nested-loop strategies, but an equality join with an additional residual condition is still eligible for equality-key strategies. Existing storage partitioning can avoid an exchange without becoming a new local matching algorithm. Statistics can be inaccurate and AQE can revise eligible strategies. There is no fixed file-size rule or promise that all five apply to a left join.
Sources: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html ; https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/v3.5.6/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/SparkStrategies.scala""",
    )

pub def slide_join_sort_merge() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="After the shuffle",
            heading="The shuffle has brought the relevant inputs together.",
            explanation="We now look inside one task, keeping the same sales and product records.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Sort the inputs",
            heading="Sort each input within its join partition.",
            explanation="This orders local keys; it does not globally sort the DataFrame.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Match G1",
            heading="Equal keys produce a joined row.",
            explanation="The task advances through the sorted inputs instead of testing every possible pair.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Keep unmatched M1",
            heading="The join type still controls unmatched rows.",
            explanation="Sorting costs CPU and may use disk when the working data exceeds memory.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="join-sort-merge",
        title="Sort-merge joins",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide join-mechanics ",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Sort-merge joins", level=1),
            group([
            step_rail(steps),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                    code("sales.join(products, on=\"product_id\", how=\"left\")"),
                ], style="language-python"),
                span("The same five sales and three products"),
            ], style="jm-query"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Aside, children=[
                    heading("After the shuffle", level=2),
                    group([
                        node(NodeKind.Strong, children=[
                            text("Worker A "),
                            node(NodeKind.Small, content="partition 0"),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Sales "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="B1"),
                            text(" / 25, 15, 10"),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Products "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="B1"),
                            text(" / books"),
                        ]),
                    ], style="jm-mini-worker"),
                    group([
                        node(NodeKind.Strong, children=[
                            text("Worker B "),
                            node(NodeKind.Small, content="partition 1"),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Sales "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="G1"),
                            text(" / 40, "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="M1"),
                            text(" / 10"),
                        ]),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Products "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="G1"),
                            text(" / games"),
                        ]),
                        span("Follow this join task", style="jm-task-focus"),
                    ], style="jm-mini-worker jm-selected"),
                    group([
                        node(NodeKind.Strong, children=[
                            text("Worker C "),
                            node(NodeKind.Small, content="partition 2"),
                        ]),
                        prose("No sales"),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Products "),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="X1"),
                            text(" / accessories"),
                        ]),
                    ], style="jm-mini-worker"),
                ], style="jm-cluster", attributes=[attr("aria-label", "The same shuffled join partitions")]),
                group([
                    group([
                        heading("Inside Worker B’s join task", level=2),
                        span("Local work on partition 1"),
                    ], style="jm-task-heading"),
                    group([
                        group([
                            heading("Sales partition", level=3),
                            group([
                                span("product_id"),
                                span("amount"),
                            ], style="jm-table-label"),
                            group([
                                group([
                                    span("G1"),
                                    span("40"),
                                ], style="jm-record jm-games jm-sort-g1"),
                                group([
                                    span("M1"),
                                    span("10"),
                                ], style="jm-record jm-missing jm-sort-m1"),
                            ], style="jm-sort-rows"),
                            prose("Arrival order is arbitrary.", style="jm-sort-before jm-only jm-p0"),
                            paragraph([
                                text("Ordered by "),
                                code("product_id"),
                            ], style="jm-sort-after jm-after-1"),
                        ], style="jm-input-sales"),
                        group([
                            heading("Products partition", level=3),
                            group([
                                span("product_id"),
                                span("category"),
                            ], style="jm-table-label"),
                            group([
                                span("G1"),
                                span("games"),
                            ], style="jm-record jm-games jm-sort-product"),
                            prose("One product row is already ordered.", style="jm-after-1"),
                        ], style="jm-input-products"),
                        group([
                            heading("Joined rows", level=3),
                            group([
                                text("What happens after"),
                                line_break(),
                                text("the records arrive?"),
                            ], style="jm-output-placeholder jm-until-2"),
                            group([
                                group([
                                    span("G1"),
                                    span("40"),
                                    span("games"),
                                ], style="jm-record jm-games jm-sort-result-g1 jm-after-2"),
                                group([
                                    span("M1"),
                                    span("10"),
                                    span("NULL"),
                                ], style="jm-record jm-missing jm-sort-result-m1 jm-after-3"),
                            ], style="jm-result-rows"),
                        ], style="jm-local-output"),
                    ], style="jm-local-columns"),
                    group([
                        prose("Both inputs are local. Their keys still need to be put in order.", style="jm-only jm-p0"),
                        prose("The task can now advance through the two ordered inputs.", style="jm-only jm-p1"),
                        paragraph([
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="G1 = G1", style="jm-key"),
                            node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                                text("Emit the sale with "),
                                strong("games"),
                                text(", then advance the sales input."),
                            ]),
                        ], style="jm-only jm-p2"),
                        paragraph([
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="M1 has no match", style="jm-key jm-missing"),
                            node(NodeKind.Span, children=[
                                text("The products input is exhausted. The left join retains the sale with "),
                                strong("NULL"),
                                text("."),
                            ]),
                        ], style="jm-only jm-p3 jm-last"),
                    ], style="jm-match-explanation"),
                ], style="jm-local-task"),
            ], style="jm-workbench"),
            step_cues(steps),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-prev", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-next", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-reset", flag=true)]),
                span("", style="lesson-position", attributes=[attr("aria-live", "polite")]),
            ], style="lesson-controls"),
        ], style="lesson jm-lesson jm-sort", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "3")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("65"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Continue the same equality left join and the exact partition allocation from the approved shuffle story. Partition 0 on A has three B1 sales and B1/books; partition 1 on B has G1/40 and M1/10 with G1/games; partition 2 on C contains only X1/accessories. The detail follows B because it contains two different sales keys. Show M1 before G1 as one possible arrival order; arrival order was never guaranteed by the earlier diagram.

First put both inputs into key order. The single product row is trivially ordered, so only the sales input visibly reorders in this tiny example. Do not invent extra product records just to make the animation bigger. Then G1 matches G1: output G1/40/games. Advance the sales stream to M1; the product stream is exhausted. Because the user's logical join is left outer, output M1/10/NULL. Worker A separately emits the three B1/books sales, and C emits none. Duplicate matching keys require handling the matching groups and may produce multiple output rows; they are not silently deduplicated. The full join still emits five rows totaling 100. Our presentation order is explanatory, not a statement about global task scheduling.

SortMergeJoin requires ordered keys and compatible child distributions; planning can insert local Sort and Exchange operators when required, or reuse suitable existing ordering/distribution. A local sort is not a global DataFrame orderBy. Sort operations can spill and incur CPU/disk work. Do not call this always Spark's default or promise that it is always slower than hashing. The plan and placement are illustrative, not runtime captures.
Sources: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html ; https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/v3.5.6/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/joins/SortMergeJoinExec.scala""",
    )

pub def slide_join_shuffled_hash() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="Same partitions",
            heading="The records reach the same join partitions as before.",
            explanation="Changing the matching algorithm does not by itself remove the required shuffle.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Build the lookup",
            heading="Build a hash table from one side of this partition.",
            explanation="For this left join, the products partition is the build side.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Probe M1",
            heading="Probe the lookup with the first sales key.",
            explanation="M1 has no entry, so the left join retains its sale with a NULL category.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Probe G1",
            heading="Probe the next key and emit its match.",
            explanation="G1 finds games without first sorting the two inputs.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Memory cost",
            heading="The trade-off is memory for the local lookup.",
            explanation="An oversized or skewed build partition can put that task under memory pressure.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="join-shuffled-hash",
        title="Shuffled hash joins",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide join-mechanics ",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
