                        ]),
                    ], style="jm-partition-grid"),
                ], style="jm-cart-grid-area"),
                node(NodeKind.Aside, children=[
                    group([
                        heading("Inputs still have partitions.", level=2),
                        prose("There is no join key here to send matching records to one destination."),
                    ], style="jm-only jm-p0"),
                    group([
                        heading("Nine partition-pair tasks", level=2),
                        prose("Each task reads one sales partition and one products partition."),
                        prose("The same input partition participates in several tasks."),
                    ], style="jm-only jm-p1"),
                    group([
                        heading("Inside Task 0", level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Both sales combine with"),
                            line_break(),
                            strong("G1 / games"),
                            text("."),
                        ]),
                        group([
                            group([
                                span("B1 / 25"),
                                span("G1 / games"),
                            ], style="jm-record  "),
                            group([
                                span("G1 / 40"),
                                span("G1 / games"),
                            ], style="jm-record  "),
                        ], style="jm-cart-result"),
                        prose("A cross join does not require the product IDs to match.", style="jm-detail-note"),
                    ], style="jm-only jm-p2"),
                    group([
                        heading("5 sales × 3 products", level=2),
                        node(NodeKind.Strong, content="15 rows", style="jm-big-count"),
                        prose("More partitions divide the work. They do not reduce the number of requested pairs."),
                    ], style="jm-only jm-p3 jm-last"),
                ], style="jm-cart-detail"),
            ], style="jm-cart-layout"),
            step_cues(steps),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-prev", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-next", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-reset", flag=true)]),
                span("", style="lesson-position", attributes=[attr("aria-live", "polite")]),
            ], style="lesson-controls"),
        ], style="lesson jm-lesson jm-cartesian", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "3")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("69"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Explicitly change the logical request to sales.crossJoin(products). Retain the original 3 sales partitions and 3 product partitions: sales P0 B1/25,G1/40; P1 B1/15,M1/10; P2 B1/10. Products P0 G1/games; P1 X1/accessories; P2 B1/books. This is an illustrative plan using CartesianProduct, also called shuffle-and-replicate nested loop in Spark join-strategy vocabulary. It pairs each left input partition with each right input partition, producing 9 partition-pair tasks. Spark can execute those tasks on available executors; the diagram does not tie one task to one permanent machine. Rows are read or buffered across repeated partition-pair work; this is not the earlier equality-key hash shuffle.

Task 0 takes 2 sales and 1 product, so emits 2 rows. Six task pairs emit 2 rows each and three emit 1, for 15 total. All unequal-key combinations are legitimate because the requested cross join has no equality predicate. This is not the earlier five-row enrichment and its amount total is not a useful sales total. CartesianProduct can also evaluate a condition for an inner-like join, in which case candidate count and emitted count differ. A crossJoin call does not guarantee this physical operator: Spark can choose a broadcast nested-loop plan when appropriate. Do not claim every range join is a Cartesian product or every cross join shuffles by a key. This slide illustrates the operator when selected. The 9 tasks and 15 rows are explicit toy arithmetic, not a timing result.
Sources: https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/v3.5.6/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/SparkStrategies.scala ; https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/v3.5.6/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/joins/CartesianProductExec.scala""",
    )

pub def slide_join_hints() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="Make the request",
            heading="A broadcast hint does not prove that broadcasting is safe.",
            explanation="The files here are prepared example inputs with the schemas shown earlier.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Inspect the plan",
            heading="explain() lets us inspect the planned strategy.",
            explanation="This excerpt illustrates what to look for. It is not a measured run or a timing claim.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Check the outcome",
            heading="A plan is evidence about planning, not a performance measurement.",
            explanation="Compare the executed behaviour and results; we will use the Spark UI in the Glue section.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="join-hints",
        title="Join hints",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide join-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Join hints", level=1),
            group([
            step_rail(steps),
            group([
                group([
                    group([
                        code_block("""from pyspark.sql import functions as F

sales = spark.read.parquet("data/sales.parquet")
products = spark.read.parquet("data/products.parquet")

enriched = sales.join(
    F.broadcast(products),
    on="product_id",
    how="left",
)
enriched.explain(mode="formatted")""", style="language-python"),
                    ]),
                    group([
                        group([
                            heading("A hint is a request.", level=2),
                            prose("Use it when you have evidence the chosen strategy is unsuitable."),
                            prose("Check lookup size, key uniqueness and executor memory first."),
                        ], style="join-state j0"),
                        group([
                            heading("Read what Spark planned.", level=2),
                            node(NodeKind.Preformatted, content="""BroadcastHashJoin
  LeftOuter · BuildRight
  BroadcastExchange""", style="join-plan"),
                            node(NodeKind.Small, content="Illustrative excerpt, not captured output"),
                            prose("Compare SortMergeJoin, ShuffledHashJoin and BroadcastHashJoin; inspect their exchanges and build side."),
                        ], style="join-state j1"),
                        group([
                            heading("Then check execution.", level=2),
                            prose("AQE may revise eligible choices when runtime evidence arrives."),
                            prose("Confirm output correctness and actual behaviour before keeping the hint."),
                        ], style="join-state j2"),
                    ]),
                ], style="join-code-layout"),
            ], style="joins-canvas"),
            step_cues(steps),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-prev", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-next", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-reset", flag=true)]),
                span("", style="lesson-position", attributes=[attr("aria-live", "polite")]),
            ], style="lesson-controls"),
        ], style="lesson joins-lesson join-hints", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "2")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("70"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="File fixtures defined in joins-example.py beside the deck; requires a configured SparkSession. Hint priority and applicability vary; Spark may not support a hinted strategy for a given join type. BROADCAST can override the automatic size threshold but cannot remove memory constraints. Do not teach hints as stronger than correctness or statistics. Static explain can show an initial adaptive plan; final executed plan may differ. The sample physical-plan labels are schematic rather than literal guaranteed output. Manual repartition can introduce avoidable work. Source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html",
    )

pub def slide_join_to_aggregation() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="The joined rows",
            heading="The join matched products. Our query also asks for category totals.",
            explanation="Follow the same five joined sales from the shuffle example.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Another books product",
            heading="Different products can belong to the same category.",
            explanation="B1 and B2 both mean books, but their joined sales are on different workers.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="A new grouping key",
            heading="Neither worker has the complete books total.",
            explanation="Joining by product_id did not bring all books sales together by category.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Another exchange",
            heading="This plan needs another exchange, now by category.",
            explanation="Before that exchange, Spark can calculate partial totals locally.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="join-to-aggregation",
        title="The join is only part of the query",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide aggregation-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("The join is only part of the query", level=1),
            group([
            step_rail(steps),
            group([
                group([
                    node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                        code("enriched = sales.join(products, on=\"product_id\", how=\"left\")"),
                    ], style="ag-join-code language-python"),
                    node(NodeKind.Preformatted, children=[
                        code("totals = enriched.groupBy(\"category\").agg(F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total\"))"),
                    ], style="ag-group-code language-python"),
                ], style="ag-code-lines"),
            ], style="ag-query"),
            group([
                group([
                    group([
                        text("Joined rows: product_id / amount / category"),
                    ], style="ag-workers-label"),
                    group([
                        heading("Worker A", level=2),
                        node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="product_id"),
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="amount"),
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="category"),
                                ]),
                            ], style="sr-only"),
                            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="B1"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="25"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                                ], style="ag-books"),
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="B1"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="15"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                                ], style="ag-books"),
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="B1"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="10"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                                ], style="ag-books"),
                            ]),
                        ], style="ag-data", attributes=[attr("aria-label", "Joined sales on Worker A")]),
                    ], style="ag-worker"),
                    group([
                        heading("Worker B", level=2),
                        node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
                            node(NodeKind.TableHead, children=[
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="product_id"),
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="amount"),
                                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="category"),
                                ]),
                            ], style="sr-only"),
                            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="G1"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="40"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="games"),
                                ], style="ag-games"),
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="M1"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="10"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="NULL"),
                                ], style="ag-null"),
                                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="B2"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="20"),
                                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="books"),
                                ], style="ag-books ag-new-sale"),
                            ]),
                        ], style="ag-data", attributes=[attr("aria-label", "Joined sales on Worker B")]),
                    ], style="ag-worker"),
                    group([
                        heading("Worker C", level=2),
                        prose("No joined sales", style="ag-empty"),
                    ], style="ag-worker"),
                ], style="ag-workers"),
                group([
                    group([
                        span("BACK TO OUR QUERY", style="ag-kicker"),
                        rich_heading([
                            text("The join has enriched"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("the sales."),
                        ], level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("We still need one total"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("for each "),
                            code("category"),
                            text("."),
                        ]),
                    ], style="ag-pause ag-pause-0"),
                    group([
                        span("ONE MORE SALE", style="ag-kicker"),
                        heading("B2 is also a book.", level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Extend the example with"),
                            line_break(),
                            strong("B2 / books / 20"),
                            text("."),
                            line_break(),
                            text("Its join output is on Worker B."),
                        ]),
                    ], style="ag-pause ag-pause-1"),
                    group([
                        span("AUDIENCE PAUSE", style="ag-kicker"),
                        rich_heading([
                            text("Who can calculate the"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("complete books total?"),
                        ], level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Worker A has the B1 sales."),
                            line_break(),
                            text("Worker B has the B2 sale."),
                        ]),
                    ], style="ag-pause ag-pause-2"),
                    group([
                        prose("Simplified physical plan, read top to bottom"),
                        group([
                            text("Join on "),
                            strong("product_id"),
                        ], style="ag-plan-step"),
                        group([
                            text("Partial sum by "),
                            strong("category"),
                        ], style="ag-plan-step"),
                        group([
                            text("Exchange by "),
                            strong("category"),
                            span("The aggregation needs a different distribution."),
                        ], style="ag-plan-step ag-plan-exchange"),
                        group([
                            text("Final sum by "),
                            strong("category"),
                        ], style="ag-plan-step"),
                    ], style="ag-plan"),
                ], style="ag-side"),
            ], style="ag-intro-layout"),
            paragraph([
                strong("Extended example:"),
                text(" the original five sales + B2 / books / 20. Worker placement is illustrative."),
            ], style="ag-context-note"),
            step_cues(steps),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-prev", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-next", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-reset", flag=true)]),
                span("", style="lesson-position", attributes=[attr("aria-live", "polite")]),
            ], style="lesson-controls"),
        ], style="lesson aggregation-lesson ag-intro", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "3")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("71"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Return to the opening chapter query after the join strategies and hints. Read the two operations separately: the join matches product_id, while groupBy asks for totals by category. Point 1 retains the exact five joined sales from the previous shuffle story, with the three B1 sales on A and G1/M1 on B. Worker C had only the unmatched X1 product, so its left join emitted no sales.

Point 2 deliberately extends the example by ONE sale: s6, product_id B2, amount20; the product directory also has the unique entry B2/books. Show the resulting joined row on Worker B. Announce both facts, rather than silently changing the data. We now have six sales totalling120. This is a fresh illustrative input extension, not a claim that a completed job incrementally accepts another row. The physical join is not replayed here. Product-id hash partitions may contain several keys, so B2 can share the shown join partition with G1 and M1. The chosen hash assignment and worker placement are illustrative, not captured Spark output.

Point 3 is the audience pause. All four books sales must contribute to one category total, but neither worker has them all. A product_id distribution is not sufficient to establish a category distribution. The tiny original example happened to place all its books on A because it had only one books product; the new B2 shows why this is not a general property.

Point 4 gives a simplified data-flow order for the physical operators: join, partial category sum, exchange by category, final category sum. It is illustrative rather than verbatim explain output; real explain prints the root at the top, and will include the join's children, possible sorting, projections, AQE wrappers and attribute IDs. Depending on the chosen strategy and existing compatible distribution, exchanges elsewhere in the plan may differ. An action on totals requests the complete query. A DataFrame variable is not an automatic materialisation boundary. Transition: do we have to send every individual sale through this next exchange?

Sources: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-syntax-qry-explain.html ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html""",
    )

pub def slide_aggregation_partials() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="Six joined sales",
            heading="The same six sales still need three category totals.",
            explanation="First ask what each worker can calculate without fetching anything.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Local partial totals",
            heading="Worker A combines 25 + 15 + 10 into a partial sum of 50.",
            explanation="Worker B contributes books 20, games 40 and NULL 10. Worker C has no sales.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Shuffle by category",
            heading="The shuffle brings partial totals for the same category together.",
            explanation="The two books partials, 50 and 20, reach the same aggregation task.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
        TeachingStep(label="Final totals",
            heading="The final aggregation combines the partials: books 70, games 40, NULL 10.",
            explanation="Local aggregation can reduce shuffle bytes. It still has to read and process the input rows.",
            cue_key="", numbered=false),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="aggregation-partials",
        title="Local totals reduce what moves",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide aggregation-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("06 / GROUPING, JOINING & AGGREGATING"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
